d  and i think they're right about that the  interest the army has been well rewarded  on the whole for the policies that  musharraf has pursued  there could well be nuanced differences  in the management of those policies by a  particular successor of musharraf and  the history of army chiefs in pakistan  is one  of occasional wild cards  uh aslam begged for instance in the  early 1990s we now understand to be one  of the root collaborators with iran on  nuclear matters  sometimes these guys come up the chain  and they sort of break out of the  of the policy  consensus that the court commanders have  been operating with or they twist it and  turn it in a new direction so you can't  rule that out but i think essentially  what musharraf represents is a  particularly  fluent  general in western idioms who is  nonetheless representing a consensus  among his  disciplined  senior leadership in the army  i think that actually  the  issue in pakistan in the medium run is  not  the  lack of a consensus among the senior  uniformed generals about  the broad outline of policies now being  pursued but whether or not the army  with creative american and indian  collaboration  will learn to share power and by sharing  power strengthening strengthen the  country and even strengthen itself as an  institution  this is the real trick  and and i don't know how good musharraf  is at that trick  like a lot of despots he's become  enamored of his own position and you can  feel that in the way that he  interacts  with  people  but  at the same time he is looking for a way  to  broaden the political base that the army  operates in he's trying now to draw the  pakistan people's party back in  to politics that would be a good thing  if he could figure out a way to do it  but he doesn't want to actually share  power with the pakistan people party's  leaders so that becomes  a hard objective to achieve and  you know there have been some generals  in pakistan's history that have been  better at domestic politics than others  and i think that more than a consensus  within the core commander group is the  challenge that musharraf now faces he  has to rise to a different kind of  politics and succeed  and i i think that  he has to do that for the army's sake  but also  for for pakistan's  what sake your take on the the new  quasi-democratically elected karzai  government is this  experiment  um  in such an islamic country  feasible is do you see this working in  the long term or  once the united states pulls out  do you see this as being something that  you know was a small chapter in afghan  history and  that's it  how do we think about the karzai  government and its longevity its  dependence on the united states what's  the nature of this experiment  i think that  um  it is  a  not an aberration  entirely in afghan history that it's a  tentative  and uh in many ways hopeful restoration  of an extension of  the centrist afghan politics that were  present in the long period of peace  between  1930 and  1970 and that karzai's  success while inseparable from american  support  now and for the foreseeable future  is not  rooted solely in his partnership with  the united states and the international  community it is also  very profoundly rooted in  afghan politics and the desire after 20  years of shattering war  of  afghans of many stripes to construct a  different  kind of politics in a different nation  and i think that  the  the dog that didn't bark  in afghanistan over the last three years  has  had opportunity to bark a number of  times and has stood down fahim  uh  the  thuggish defense minister and  intelligence chief and partner of ahmed  shah massoud who locked out of the  spoils of the cabinet  accepted  his  fate without trying to organize a coup  d'etat  canooni another  disappointed member of the cabinet  decides to start a political party and  not to point a tank barrel at his  adversaries  dostum  ismail khan  made the poster boy of the warlords who  have to go  he accepts ministry of minds and energy  or whatever it is he's got now you know  good for him uh it's probably a pretty  lucrative ministry as these things go  not to cast aspersions on his integrity  but i mean it's a it's a you know it's  the kind of normalization of politics  that's essential if this is going to  stick and it's happening it's happening  not because  the americans want it to happen it's  happening because afghans are making  this decision time and again  why did the taliban not turn up in  november yes the united states  delivered the security regime  with the resources it had that was  necessary yes so did nato and the united  nations and others collaborated  successfully on the role that outsiders  could play  but the taliban didn't show up because  ordinary pashtuns made it impossible for  them to operate ordinary pashtuns  essentially rejected the taliban as a  vehicle for pashtun aspirations  what's what's the most unfinished  business in this respect  it's pashtun politics  if  afghanistan can organize  over the next five to ten years  with the space that the international  communities and american  policy creates  ordinary pashtun politics that resembles  the decision-making i referred to in the  northern alliance which was a mature  political movement  in a sense  then i really then there's really hope  right now  the big problem  in a sense if you wanted to reduce it  in afghanistan politically is that if i  me if hamid karzai were to be run over  by a bus tomorrow  there is no committee of pashtun  political leaders who would meet to  choose his credible successor and whose  decision would be regarded as credible  among the broad range of pashtun  constituents  that's the moment afghanistan has to  arrive at that there that  that this is not all about hamid karzai  anymore that it is about parties  political parties that represent diverse  pashtun points of view  that are engaged in constitutional  politics and the parliamentary elections  are very important in that respect  because they will produce the basis for  such politics but then it has to be  actually created and it'll take  some time  um  but anyway i feel overall uh optimistic  it's getting colder and colder in here  the woman with the glove  speaks to me  for extending the fragile  optimism that you have in afghanistan to  the iraqi election  yeah is there is there a basis for  similar optimism in iraq not not visible  i think that the the most interesting  i mean obviously you all understand as  well as i do uh what's happening in iraq  because i haven't walked around that  insurgency  and you know i've been there  once uh and and really don't have a rich  confidence about  the insurgency  but i  i do  have a couple of thoughts one is that i  think the  if you ask the question as i sometimes  try to do what's happening in iraq  that matters that we understand the  least  and in the region in a way as a result  of  what's happening in iraq and i think it  one answer to that is the shia revival  there is a very powerful  moment  in  shia communities  in the arab world and globally that  is  at the heart of what's going to unfold  this week in iraq  the other side of the  coin  on the other side of the sunnis  insurgency bathurst nationalist  insurgency  is  a community's commitment to this  constitutional process yes out of  self-interest but also as part of a  broader regional revival a moment of  enfranchisement for a community that has  felt profoundly disenfranchised not only  in iraq but elsewhere in the gulf i was  talking to somebody who was in saudi  um talking to folks about the these  municipal elections that are due in a  couple of weeks that are you know they  don't amount to a whole lot but they're  something to measure and they're  interesting  and  he was out in riyadh  asking who's registering to vote  and  in riyadh the answer was not very many  people so far  but apparently in the eastern  areas around dahran where the shias are  prominent registration is running at  very very high rates  you know why well because there's an  echo from  what's happening in iraq and  uh  so i i think that  if you try to look at these elections  from an iraqi  rather than an american point of view  those are some of the things you might  observe where it leads  involves more than we want to talk about  tonight but  um  but the violence and the breadth  and  complexity of the  sunni-based insurgency  has no analog in afghanistan mercifully  and doesn't look easily addressed by  these elections  i'm  generally working in the same territory  as this last one but i'm i'm going to  work a little more on on saudi arabia  this next time around it's going to be a  long time before you see the fruits of  that work  that's about as much as i'm prepared to  say thank you though for your interest  yes  of our relationship with the northern  alliance from september 10th  to september 12th  especially considering how much trust we  placed in them  in the war on terrorism in those  following months i mean there's it's  been reported that there were  bounties paid to them for prisoners that  they turned over to us who may or may  not have ended up in guantanamo bay and  i was wondering how strong that  relationship was before  september 11th well i think  part of what i tried to document and  narrate was  that  on september 10th there was an  unresolved and unfinished well it was  resolved but not in the favor of those  on the northern alliance side of the  argument there was an argument about  whether or not the northern alliance  should be seen as an ally of the united  states or not  and  particularly  at the cia in the two years before  september 11th a forceful case was made  from the working level not always made  at the leadership level  that we really ought to choose sides in  this afghan war and that was the only  serious way to confront bin laden and  the only way to choose sides was to 