ickly that i wanted to be  a foreign correspondent the way it works  at the post is it's a very  you know it's like a lot of other places  very competitive to try to get a foreign  correspondence job and so when you want  to apply  you have to essentially declare that  you're willing to go anywhere so that's  what i did i said that i was willing to  go anywhere anytime you know  and  so in the course of uh being appointed  to that staff  i thought i was going lots of different  places and in fact  not to tell a boring story about myself  but it was kind of a moment when i was  still am married had young kids and  my wife was very adventurous wanted was  willing to apply basically a blank sheet  of paper but  we had been told we've been led to  believe in the course of this  application process that we were going  first we were going to south africa then  we were going to poland and i was called  down to a meeting in washington we were  living in new york  right on the cusp of our making our  final commitments and i go into a  meeting with my boss and he starts  talking to me about okay you're gonna  this is how the moving works and these  are the logistics and so forth and about  20 minutes into the conversation i  realized that he was assuming that i was  going to india which had never been  mentioned by anyone at any stage along  the way  and i i didn't want to seem like  reluctant uh to go or or sort of like i  had any second thoughts about going to  because they seemed to have already made  the appointment  but i asked if i could just like take 10  minutes just have a break and i went out  and i called home and said  how do you feel about going to india and  that was it was okay so that's how i i  went and when i was there  afghanistan was  a a big part of what the washington post  was interested in that part of the world  and i became very interested in it as i  traveled in afghanistan and discovered  it from um through my own experience i  just became more and more involved in  the region and you know i have to say  we were just back in india for christmas  and i think south asia is just one of  those places that for some people it  just kind of takes hold of you and never  really lets go and i've now  worked just about everywhere in one way  or another and  this is still the place that i feel  the most involved in as you can  undoubtedly tell  me on how  america's relationship with israel has  provided popular support for the  jihadists around the world how has  america's relationship with israel  provided popular support for jihadism  around the world  well i mean obviously the unresolved  conflict between  israelis and palestinians  is an essential part of  the context in which  a substantial  part of  radical of islamic radicalization occurs  and so i don't think you can  sort of say oh well  it's a sidebar it's context  what's interesting about bin laden i  think  though  is that he has never really been very  seriously interested in the  israeli-palestinian conflict  even rhetorically he came to it quite  late  and his own  engagement with it  was  you know was almost like a speechwriter  started to put it into his remarks after  a certain point he did have one personal  connection uh in his relationship with  abdullah azam during the 1980s abdullah  zam palestinian muslim brother who was  the founder of a precursor organization  of al qaeda and so it's not as if he  wasn't acquainted with the issues and he  did advocate for american boycotts and  anti-israeli boycotts in the 1980s as  part of his speech making so i mean to  be fair to him you know he he did speak  about the issue but as he independently  even early but as he independently built  up al qaeda after azam's assassination  in 1989 and as he defined  al-qaeda's global campaign he moved  increasingly away from  rhetorical or other concerns involving  the israeli-palestinian conflict it's  notable that  to date  so far as we know  no cells  that we would 